  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday Night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1140 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nThe ridge will strengthen further on Tuesday and Wednesday with \nhigh temperatures responding and increasing into the upper 90s and\nlower 100s. Dewpoints will also increase during this time and \nrising heat indices may warrant heat headlines for portions of \nthe area. The Tulsa metro area and AR River Valley are most at \nrisk for HIs greater than 105 degrees Tuesday and Wednesday. Dry \nconditions are likely Tuesday and Wednesday, though a few isolated\nshowers cannot be ruled out Wednesday afternoon across the higher\nterrain of SE OK and W AR as deeper moisture is pulled northward.\nMeanwhile, north of the ridge, broad troughing will develop \nacross the northern Plains through mid week. As a series of \nimpulses round the base of the trough, a weak cold front will be \npushed into the central plains and then into northern portions of \nour area late Wednesday. \n\nThe front will tend to increase precipitation potential across NE\nOK and NW AR through late week, especially while ridging is \nsuppressed as another shortwave or two influence the region \nThursday into Friday. While model solutions vary at this range, \nhighest rain/storm potential is currently expected to remain along\nand north of I-40 late this week. Any storms will likely pose a \nthreat for locally heavy rainfall and strong wind gusts. Heights \nincrease somewhat again this weekend into early next week, though \nlow diurnal rain/storm chances may persist with deeper moisture in\nplace. Temperatures and associated heat risk through the end of \nthe forecast period will depend on placement of the frontal \nboundary and precipitation coverage, but potential for dangerous \nheat will likely persist into this weekend. Long range guidance \ncontinues to suggest above average temperatures and near to below\naverage precipitation are favored through mid month.",
